# Tabs

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Tabs let people switch between a few clearly named groups of information, with the current one always shown. Home Assistant uses them as the view bar across the top of a dashboard, and in editors and settings pages.

In Home Assistant, tabs switch views or pages. They do not hold panels of content, and a dashboard card cannot hold tabs. To split content inside a view, see the alternatives below.

This page covers:

- Tabs
  - `ha-tab-group`
    - `ha-tab`
    - `ha-tab-panel`
  - `ha-md-tabs`
    - `ha-md-primary-tab`
    - `ha-md-secondary-tab`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A tab bar is a row of labels, one for each view or page, with a line under the current one. The current tab has a 2-pixel underline and stronger text, so its state does not rest on colour alone. When the bar is too long for the screen, it scrolls and shows scroll buttons.

The bar is not a navigation menu for a whole site. Route navigation across a site belongs on the [Navigation](navigation.html) page. Dashboard views are Home Assistant’s own routes, and the view bar is its way of showing them.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-tab-group"><code>ha-tab-group</code></h3>

The tab bar that Home Assistant draws at the top of a dashboard, and in the automation trace pages, the card editors, the dashboard dialogs and the tools panel. It is built on the Web Awesome tab group.

- **On a dashboard,** each tab is a view. You control them with the dashboard’s `views` list, using `title` and `icon` for each view and `path` for its address. You cannot add other tabs.
- **Keyboard:** the left and right arrow keys move between tabs, Home and End go to the first and last, and Enter or Space selects. Home Assistant stops Alt and Meta with an arrow from being used, so the browser’s back and forward still work.
- **Roles:** the bar is a tab list, each tab is a tab with its selected state.
- **Options:** a tab bar can place its tabs top, bottom, start or end, and activate a tab on focus or on Enter. Home Assistant’s own bars set no panels.

**What the Civica theme sets.** In the view bar, the header’s background and white text colours come from the theme (`app-header-background-color` and `app-header-text-color`). The background follows the theme’s primary colour: blue in Civica and Civica Blue, then green, teal, purple, magenta, red and black. The indicator is white and the track is transparent. Elsewhere, the active tab text and the indicator use the theme’s primary colour and the track uses the divider colour. The theme sets none of the tab-specific keys, so Home Assistant’s defaults apply.

The view bar is the app header, not a card, so the card-mod integration cannot restyle it.

<h3 id="child-ha-tab-group--ha-tab"><code>ha-tab</code></h3>

`ha-tab` is a different element from the tab inside `ha-tab-group`. The tab inside `ha-tab-group` is called `ha-tab-group-tab`. It has a label, a `panel` name, and `active` and `disabled` states.

`ha-tab` is the simpler tab used in the tab bar of settings pages. Each one is a link to a page, marked as the current page, so it behaves as a list of links rather than as ARIA tabs. It has an icon slot, an `active` state and a `narrow` state. It can show a short badge beside its name, such as the number of waiting updates. In the narrow layout, the badge becomes a dot on the icon, and its text is hidden from sight but still read as part of the link’s name. The active tab uses the theme’s primary colour with a 2-pixel underline, except in the narrow layout.

<h3 id="child-ha-tab-group--ha-tab-panel"><code>ha-tab-panel</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-tab-panel`. Its tab bars switch views or pages and do not hold content panels. The Web Awesome library that Home Assistant builds on has a tab panel (`wa-tab-panel`), but Home Assistant does not use it. The page that a tab opens holds the content. To show panels of content inside a view, use one of these:

- A [Conditional Card](conditional-card.html) driven by a helper and a [Button Group / Segmented Control](button-group-segmented-control.html).
- Headed sections stacked in one card (see [Grid & Stack Layout Cards](grid-and-stack-layout-cards.html)).
- A separate dashboard view for each group, which gives real tabs.

<h3 id="child-ha-md-tabs"><code>ha-md-tabs</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-md-tabs`. It draws tabs with `ha-tab-group` and `ha-tab`, described above.

<h3 id="child-ha-md-tabs--ha-md-primary-tab"><code>ha-md-primary-tab</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-md-primary-tab`. Its tabs have a single style, an underline on the active tab, so there is no primary style to choose. See `ha-tab-group` above.

<h3 id="child-ha-md-tabs--ha-md-secondary-tab"><code>ha-md-secondary-tab</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-md-secondary-tab`. There is no secondary style. To show a lower level of grouping, use a heading inside the view, such as the Heading card (see [Heading](heading.html)).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The view bar shows every view at once and marks the current one, so people always know where they are.
- The tab bar scrolls when it is too long, with scroll buttons.
- Home Assistant’s tabs switch views and pages. A dashboard card cannot hold tabs or panels.
- The bar has one style, so it cannot show two levels.
- Tab text and the indicator are small. Test them in your own setup, in light and dark modes.
- Home Assistant offers variables for the tab indicator, track and active text, but the theme does not set them, so the defaults apply.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use tabs for a few groups that people switch between often. Keep labels short and in the same order each time. Put the most useful first. Do not use tabs for a sequence of steps, or when people need to compare two groups side by side. Do not hide an urgent device condition in a tab that is not selected. Show it above the tabs.

An empty group should say why it is empty and keep its tab, rather than disabling it. When data refreshes, keep the tab that people chose, and do not reset their position.

Success criteria that apply:

- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value and 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: each tab has a name and a state, and its content is tied to it.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard, 2.4.3 Focus Order and 2.4.7 Focus Visible: test arrow keys, Home and End in your own setup.
- 1.4.1 Use of Color and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: the current tab is shown by an underline and stronger text as well as colour. Test the indicator against its background.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the bar scrolls at 320 CSS pixels. Test it.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): test each tab by touch.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: each panel has a useful heading.

Few tabs, short labels, a steady order and a visible current tab keep the effort low. Follow the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html) at 320 CSS pixels, with an empty panel and at 200% text.

